fact WorkItem(id: "WI-791", description: some(value: "The arrow PARAM SLOT does not record parameter-list ARITY, so a one-tuple-parameter operation is the same term as an n-parameter one. This is WI-782 case 3 (SLOT COLLAPSE), deferred from WI-782 with a MEASURED reason. REPRO (loads clean, traps at eval, unchanged on main): 'operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a' with 'let f: (_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64 = get_a' then 'f((7, 8))' traps Internal(field_access: tuple has no component 'a'); the dual, a 2-param op into 'let f: (t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = two', traps ArityMismatch{expected:2,got:1}. Both are LOUD traps, not silent wrong answers -- which is why WI-782 shipped its ORDER/ARITY fixes without this one. ROOT CAUSE: two producers collapse an arity-1 parameter list to the bare parameter type -- 'operation_as_function_value' (kb/typing.rs, 'if op.params.len() == 1') and the TypeExpr::Arrow arm of kb/load.rs ('if params.len() == 1') -- so '(t: (a: A, b: B)) -> R' and '(a: A, b: B) -> R' build the identical arrow(param = named_tuple[A, B]). WHAT WAS TRIED AND WHY IT FAILED (do not repeat it): a CONDITIONAL wrapper at the mint -- wrap the arity-1 list iff its sole parameter's type is itself a tuple, giving 'named_tuple[(v, T)]', with a decoder in arrow_parts undoing it. It was implemented, and the full workspace went green, but review + driving found it LEAKS, because the wrap is decided from the SPELLING at mint time while the slot is read back AFTER substitution: (a) MEASURED -- 'operation apply1[T](f: (x: T) -> Int64, v: T) -> Int64 = f(v)' called as 'apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2))' bound T to the WRAPPER, so the sibling parameter rejected the literal with 'expected (_1: (a: Int64, b: Int64)), got (a: Int64, b: Int64)' -- a program that works on main; (b) MEASURED -- the wrapper keys its field by the written binder name, which an arity-1 slot previously dropped, so '(t: (a,b)) -> R' stopped conforming to '(u: (a,b)) -> R' (fixed by making ParamList ignore names, which WI-782 shipped); (c) a param slot written '(x: T) -> R' or '(c: r.T) -> Bool' is NOT wrapped at mint (its TypeExpr is not a Tuple) but BECOMES a named_tuple after substitution/projection elimination, at which point it reads as an n-parameter list -- four independent review angles identified this path in stdlib mapElems/where/optionMap shapes; I could not build a working fixture for it, so it is UNCONFIRMED, but it is the same mechanism as (a), which IS confirmed. The lesson: an encoding that unification can introduce or erase is not an invariant. FIX DIRECTION (the sigma-stable alternative): make the param slot UNIFORMLY the parameter list at every arity -- '(v: Int64) -> R' carries 'named_tuple[(v, Int64)]' -- so arity is a structural property substitution can neither create nor destroy, and no conditional test is needed at any producer. Cost: it changes the term for EVERY arrow in the codebase and must be reconciled with 'Function[A, B]', whose A is ONE tuple-typed ARGUMENT and carries no list (WI-775); every reader must then be classified as asking for the parameter LIST or the single ARGUMENT type (a full map of both exists in the WI-782 session). Consider also whether the arrow node should carry arity as its own child instead. ACCEPTANCE: the two repro programs above are REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic (or otherwise made to work), driven end-to-end; 'apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2))' and the alpha-rename case still load and evaluate; a generic callback whose param type is instantiated to a tuple ('(x: T) -> R' with T := a tuple) is driven end-to-end in BOTH directions; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T12:44:32Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-791", author: "user", content: "MEASURED against the WI-782 follow-up commit: the admissibility gate restored there does NOT close this ticket, and what it leaves open is the IDIOMATIC spelling. Both cases driven end-to-end with a fresh interpreter per case. (1) POSITIONAL-SPELLING BYPASS: operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a, passed to operation take2(f: (Int64, Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(1, 2), LOADS CLEAN and traps ArityMismatch{expected: 1, got: 2} at eval; the control with the callback spelled (p: Int64, q: Int64) is correctly REFUSED at load. Cause: a positionally-written tuple type mints its components as _1, _2 via intern_positional_label (parse/convert.rs), which is exactly the synthetic shape is_positional_tuple_names admits, so the gate disarms itself on the shorter and more common spelling. (2) LEADING-ZERO BYPASS: the same program with the tuple spelled (_01: Int64, _02: Int64) also LOADS CLEAN and traps identically, because parse::<usize>() accepts leading zeros. That also puts the typer at odds with eval, where is_synthetic_positional_label (eval/eval.rs) guards on the digits not starting with a zero and WI-786 shipped leading_zero_label_is_not_synthetic asserting that _01 is a USER label. So the typer calls _01 synthetic while eval calls it a user label: the concrete cross-boundary divergence WI-789 and WI-790 predict, now observed. BOTH BYPASSES ARE PRE-EXISTING, not regressions of WI-782: is_positional_tuple_names on the follow-up commit is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the pre-WI-782 one (verified by git show against 528f4a92), so both holes are equally present on WI-782 parent. What WI-782 and its follow-up actually settled for this ticket is only the NAME-DISJOINT case. A NOT-YET-TRIED FIX DIRECTION, worth weighing against the uniform-parameter-list route already recorded here: carry ARITY on the arrow node as its own child, a sibling of param, result and effects. Both producers know the true arity at mint (op.params.len() in operation_as_function_value, params.len() in the TypeExpr::Arrow arm of load.rs) and arrow_parts already reads named children, so it is a mechanical addition. Critically, the reason the earlier wrapper attempt failed does NOT apply here: that wrapper lived INSIDE the param slot, so a type variable could bind to it and the encoding leaked through substitution. An arity child sits OUTSIDE the type that substitution acts on, so sigma can neither introduce nor erase it, which is the very invariant this ticket records as its lesson. Cost is one extra child rather than changing the term for every arrow in the codebase.", at: "2026-07-20T11:25:07Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-791", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. The arrow gained a fourth child, `arity` -- the parameter-list LENGTH as written, carried as a ground Const(Int) sibling of param/result/effects. That placement IS the fix: the reverted WI-782 wrapper lived INSIDE the param slot, so a type variable could bind to it and sigma both introduced and erased the encoding; a ground count is not a type sigma acts on. Cost was one child, not the uniform-parameter-list route (which would have changed the term for every arrow and still had to be reconciled with Function[A,B]).\n\nTWO HALVES. (1) agreed_arrow_arity: two arrows relate only at EQUAL ARITY -- one equality, replacing every name-shape heuristic. (2) the count also selects WHICH relation the param slot gets: arity != 1 => the slot IS the list (TupleAlign::ParamList, WI-782's rules unchanged); arity 1 => the slot is the sole parameter's TYPE, so a tuple there is DATA and relates BY NAME with width subtyping. Half (2) repays the cost WI-782 knowingly paid.\n\nMEASURED end-to-end with a fresh interpreter per case; the pre-fix side re-measured in an isolated git worktree at 20e4eb77. FIXED (all four loaded clean and trapped pre-fix, all four now REFUSED AT LOAD): both ticket repros; the POSITIONAL-SPELLING bypass take2(f: (Int64, Int64) -> Int64); the LEADING-ZERO bypass (_01/_02). None of the four is refused by a better name test -- each is refused because 1 != 2, which is why both bypasses close without touching the WI-789/WI-790 _N divergence. RESTORED (refused pre-fix, now load and evaluate to 7): permuted and narrower tuple parameter -- WI-782's two knowing false-rejects. UNCHANGED: apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2)) (the program the wrapper broke), the alpha-rename case, foldLeft eta, the Function spelling, scalar arity-1.\n\nDIAGNOSTICS. The two spellings used to RENDER IDENTICALLY, so a rejection would have restated the confusion it reported. type_display_name / type_display_name_occ / TermPrinter now wrap an arity-1 tuple param: \"expected (p: Int64, q: Int64) -> Int64, got ((a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64\". The print round trip is FAITHFUL and a FIXPOINT -- pinned by a test that reparses its own output and prints a second generation, because the reverted wrapper gained a layer per round until generation three no longer parsed.\n\nONE DELIBERATE BEHAVIOR CHANGE, pinned as two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow: take(f: ((Int64, Int64)) -> Int64) given a 2-param op LOADED and evaluated to -1 pre-fix, because eval's spread_eta_args spreads a single POSITIONAL tuple argument across a multi-parameter op. Refused now. It is the SAME relation as repro 2, which the acceptance requires refusing; the two only looked different because repro 2's tuple is NAME-keyed and so has nothing in .pos for the spread to find. Letting the relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is the incoherence this ticket removes. The spread convention is untouched and still reachable through Function[(A,B),R] (driven, = 7) and a direct positional call.\n\nWHY NO ARITY CHECK ON THE arrow-vs-Function ARM: Function states no arity and cannot -- its A is the ONE argument apply(f, x: A) passes, yet Function[(T,T),R] has ALSO denoted a 2-param op's eta arrow since WI-775. Driven both ways and pinned. The spec's Function note is updated: the permutation/width half of that disagreement is RESOLVED (both spellings now relate a lone tuple parameter by name); what remains is only that Function admits either arity.\n\nLAMBDA ARITY = the WRITTEN binder count (Pattern::Tuple component count, else 1), NOT the runtime's. enter_closure is strictly unary, so a multi-binder lambda into an n-parameter callback still traps (WI-784). Minting 1 would convert that trap into a LOAD rejection -- measured as the only alternative, and left to WI-784 rather than taken here. Note the surface has NO spelling for \"one tuple-shaped binder\" other than naming it: WI-620 made outer parens a grouping node, so `lambda ((a,b)) ->` and `lambda (a,b) ->` are the same lambda.\n\nKNOWN GAP LEFT OPEN AND PINNED (known_gap_generic_callback_arrow_is_not_conformance_checked): a callback argument to a TYPE-PARAMETERIZED op is not conformance-checked at all, so apply2[T](f: (x: T, y: T) -> Int64) given a 1-tuple-param op still loads clean and traps. MEASURED IDENTICAL pre-fix -- PRE-EXISTING, not a hole in the arity child (the same program written non-generically IS refused). ROOT CAUSE for whoever closes it: validate_arg_against_param gates on resolved_type_is_ground for BOTH sides, and a declared (x: T, y: T) -> Int64 is non-ground while T is free, so the full types_compatible -- where the arity check lives -- never runs; the non-ground path delegates to validate_arrow_param_result, which checks param/result component-wise and only where each component is itself ground. ARITY IS THE ONE COMPONENT THAT CHECK COULD ALWAYS DECIDE: it is a ground Const(Int) however polymorphic the types are, so the groundness discipline that defers the rest does not apply to it. Not done here because it also rejects programs that work today via eval's tuple spread -- WI-784 / WI-792 territory, same family as WI-792.\n\nFOOTGUN THE /code-review CAUGHT, worth keeping: adding a REQUIRED child SILENTLY EMPTIED TWO TESTS. types_compatible_bootstrap_safe_when_prelude_not_registered and subtype_rejects_malformed_multi_tail_row hand-build raw Term::Fn{Arrow, named} to control their effects child; with no arity child agreed_arrow_arity returned false FIRST, so neither test reached a single one of the effect-row arms it names -- and both stayed GREEN. Fixed by giving them the child (make_arity_term is now pub for exactly this), and made loud by a debug_assert in agreed_arrow_arity: both callers arrive from an (arrow, arrow) dispatch arm, so a side stating no arity is a MALFORMED term, not a legitimate absence. The full suite passing in debug with that assert live is the completeness check on the producer map.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh: 3172 passed, 0 failed. New suite wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs (12 tests) verified in an isolated worktree to FAIL pre-fix for all 9 changed behaviours while EVERY control passes pre-fix, so they discriminate rather than track. wi782's three known_gap_* tests are retired: two became positive end-to-end assertions, the third became this ticket's headline rejection. docs/kernel-language.md states the shipped rule; docs/proposals/typing_pass_spec.anthill updated so its arrow patterns still match.", at: "2026-07-20T12:44:31Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-791", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION to my delivery feedback: the new suite wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs has ELEVEN tests, not twelve. Everything else in that note stands, including the pre-fix worktree measurement (nine changed behaviours FAIL pre-fix across wi791 + wi782 while every control passes) and the 3172-passed workspace figure.", at: "2026-07-20T13:09:48Z")
